WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.013
Bayes' theorem reverses a conditional probability by accounting for how common each group is

00:00:06.013 --> 00:00:11.225
Imagine one hundred objects: twenty are type A and eighty are type B

00:00:11.225 --> 00:00:14.833
A screening rule flags some objects from either group.

00:00:16.133 --> 00:00:21.138
The rule flags ninety percent of type A and ten percent of type B

00:00:21.138 --> 00:00:26.501
In this frequency model, that means eighteen flagged A objects and eight flagged B objects

00:00:26.501 --> 00:00:30.433
The remaining objects are not part of the flagged comparison group.

00:00:31.733 --> 00:00:36.541
Once a flag is observed, compare only the twenty-six flagged objects

00:00:36.541 --> 00:00:41.348
Eighteen are type A, giving nine thirteenths, or about sixty-nine percent

00:00:41.348 --> 00:00:47.467
The original ninety percent described flags within type A, not type A within flags.

00:00:48.767 --> 00:00:54.485
Now make type A rarer: ten of a thousand objects, with the same flagging rates

00:00:54.485 --> 00:00:58.678
There are nine flagged A objects and ninety-nine flagged B objects

00:00:58.678 --> 00:01:03.633
The posterior falls to one twelfth, showing why base rates cannot be ignored.
